  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nAn amplified longwave pattern featuring ridging across the high \nplains and a deep trough over the Great Lakes and northeast with \nnortherly flow across Wisconsin, resulting in cool, seasonable \ntemps. Scattered lake effect showers continue across northern \nWisconsin and along parts of the Lake Michigan shoreline, but expect \nimpacts to be minimal. These showers will diminish this evening as \nhigh pressure approaches from the west. \n\nHeading through the weekend, surface ridging will spread across the \nUpper Midwest with dry weather, relatively light winds, and \nseasonable temperatures.\n\nFor next week, mid level ridging will build across the Midwest ahead \nof a broad trough overspreading the western US. On the backside of \nthe departing high, southerly low-level flow will develop, with a \nwarming trend expected through late in the week. Confidence is high \nfor above average temps, and the spread in high temperatures is \nrelatively low through mid-week. Highs likely will reach the 50s, \nwith NBM 50th-75th percentile max temps reaching the low 60s for\nsome areas later next week.\n\nConfidence for dry weather is also very high through Wednesday under \nthe influence of the ridging. By late next week, southwesterly flow \nbecomes established, with global ensemble means showing precipitable \nwater values exceeding the 90th percentile. Precipitation chances \n(30-40%) return by Thursday, but the warmer temperatures \nsuggest rain as the likely precipitation type through this \nperiod.",
